WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:01.820
- [Narrator] Imagine remembering

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.860
every single day of your life,

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.563
all in perfect detail.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.630
For those with highly superior
autobiographical memory,

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.500
or H.S.A.M for short,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.183
this is their reality.

00:00:19.487 --> 00:00:20.690
- [Rebecca] I remember everything

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:25.690
that's personally happened
to me since I was born.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.320
- [Narrator] That's Rebecca Sharrock.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.080
I was fascinated by this condition,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.617
so I called her up to find out more.

00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:33.960
(phone rings)

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:34.810
Hi, Rebecca.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:35.810
- [Rebecca] Hi!

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:36.918
- [Narrator] How are you going?

00:00:36.918 --> 00:00:38.253
- [Rebecca] I'm doing alright.

00:00:38.253 --> 00:00:41.670
It's a very wet Saturday morning here.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.250
How are you going?

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.730
- [Narrator] Side note, in this moment,

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.770
I just realized that Rebecca will remember

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.023
that this day was a wet morning forever.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.928
So, you remember
everything in great detail.

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:57.710
- [Rebecca] People can remember
what they did last Saturday,

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:02.710
but I can remember what I
did Saturday 10 years ago.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.270
- [Narrator] What about a memory

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.330
from when you were just a baby?

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.320
- [Rebecca] When I was 12 days old,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.130
I was lying down on the fur car seat

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.860
in my parent's car,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.420
and I was staring up
at the steering wheel,

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.050
and I thought, “What is that?"

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.810
- [Narrator] Rebecca is one
of only 60 people worldwide

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.210
to have been identified
with having H.S.A.M

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.530
- [Rebecca] All through my teenage years,

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.310
I thought that everybody
remembered in this kind of way.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.360
I thought it was completely normal.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.240
- [Narrator] It wasn't until she was 21,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.530
when her parents decided to submit her

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.010
to the university of California

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.100
to undergo two years of testing.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.513
After this, she was officially
identified as having H.S.A.M.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.310
Since memories can come
to mind unexpectedly,

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.370
any time of day,

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:50.820
a negative memory like this

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.290
can make living with H.S.A.M difficult.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:57.400
- [Rebecca] Mom was putting
me in this itchy satin dress,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.680
and I was crying because I didn't like it,

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.610
and mom gave me this Minnie Mouse toy,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.030
and I couldn't say anything at the time,

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.740
but her face terrified me.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.940
- [Narrator] But on most
days H.S.A.M is a gift.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.230
Rebecca can recall happy
memories and moments

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.000
when times get tough.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.200
- [Rebecca] A good memory
that makes me so thankful

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.713
about having this kind of H.S.A.M memory.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.070
- [Narrator] Thanks for chatting Rebecca.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.740
- [Rebecca] Yup, thanks.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.073
- [Narrator] Bye.

